<section xml:id="sec-f-2-integral-formulas">
  <title>Integral formulas</title>

  <introduction><p>An indefinite integral is an antiderivative family:</p>

  <p><me>\int f(x)\,dx=F(x)+C</me></p>

  <p>means</p>

  <p><me>F'(x)=f(x).</me></p>

  <p>A definite integral is accumulated signed amount:</p>

  <p><me>\int_a^b f(x)\,dx.</me></p>

  <p>The two are connected by the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus. Apply the tables on intervals where every expression is defined. For definite integral rules, assume the integrands are continuous; for substitution assume <m>g</m> has a continuous derivative, and for integration by parts assume <m>u</m> and <m>v</m> have continuous derivatives. Antiderivative constants are chosen separately on disconnected domain intervals.</p>

  </introduction><subsection xml:id="appsubsec-general-integral-rules">
    <title>General integral rules</title>

  <table xml:id="tab-sec-f-2-integral-formulas-0">
    <title>General integral rules</title><tabular halign="left" width="100%">
      <col width="50.0%"/><col width="50.0%"/><row header="yes" bottom="medium">
        <cell><p><alert>rule</alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p><alert>formula</alert></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Linearity</p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>\displaystyle
\int \bigl(af(x)+bg(x)\bigr)\,dx
=
a\int f(x)\,dx+b\int g(x)\,dx</m></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Definite linearity</p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>\displaystyle
\int_a^b \bigl(af(x)+bg(x)\bigr)\,dx
=
a\int_a^b f(x)\,dx+b\int_a^b g(x)\,dx</m></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Orientation</p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>\displaystyle
\int_b^a f(x)\,dx=-\int_a^b f(x)\,dx</m></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Zero interval</p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>\displaystyle
\int_a^a f(x)\,dx=0</m></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Additivity over intervals</p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>\displaystyle
\int_a^b f(x)\,dx+\int_b^c f(x)\,dx=\int_a^c f(x)\,dx</m></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Fundamental Theorem</p></cell>
        <cell><p>If <m>F'=f</m>, then
<m>\displaystyle
\int_a^b f(x)\,dx=F(b)-F(a)</m></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Substitution</p></cell>
        <cell><p>If <m>u=g(x)</m>, then
<m>\displaystyle
\int f(g(x))g'(x)\,dx=\int f(u)\,du</m></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Definite substitution</p></cell>
        <cell><p>If <m>u=g(x)</m>, then
<m>\displaystyle
\int_a^b f(g(x))g'(x)\,dx
=
\int_{g(a)}^{g(b)} f(u)\,du</m></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Integration by parts</p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>\displaystyle
\int u\,dv=uv-\int v\,du</m></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p>Definite integration by parts</p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>\displaystyle
\int_a^b u\,dv=\bigl[uv\bigr]_a^b-\int_a^b v\,du</m></p></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <note xml:id="note-sec-f-2-integral-formulas-11">
    <title>Check by differentiating</title>
      <p>For an indefinite integral, the fastest way to check an answer is to differentiate it.
The derivative should return the original integrand.</p>
  </note>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="appsubsec-basic-antiderivatives">
    <title>Basic antiderivatives</title>

  <table xml:id="tab-sec-f-2-integral-formulas-1">
    <title>Basic antiderivatives</title><tabular halign="left" width="100%">
      <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
        <cell><alert>integrand</alert></cell>
        <cell><alert>antiderivative</alert></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\displaystyle x^r,\quad r\neq -1</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle \int x^r\,dx=\frac{x^{r+1}}{r+1}+C</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\displaystyle \frac1x</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle \int \frac1x\,dx=\ln|x|+C</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\displaystyle e^x</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle \int e^x\,dx=e^x+C</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\displaystyle e^{kx}</m>, <m>k\neq0</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle \int e^{kx}\,dx=\frac1k e^{kx}+C</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\displaystyle a^x,\quad a&gt;0,\ a\neq1</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle \int a^x\,dx=\frac{a^x}{\ln a}+C</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle \frac{f'(x)}{f(x)}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle \int \frac{f'(x)}{f(x)}\,dx=\ln|f(x)|+C</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="appsubsec-trig-antiderivatives">
    <title>Trigonometric antiderivatives</title>

  <table xml:id="tab-sec-f-2-integral-formulas-2">
    <title>Trigonometric antiderivatives</title><tabular halign="left" width="100%">
      <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
        <cell><alert>integrand</alert></cell>
        <cell><alert>antiderivative</alert></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\sin x</m></cell>
        <cell><m>-\cos x+C</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\cos x</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\sin x+C</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\sec^2 x</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\tan x+C</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\csc^2 x</m></cell>
        <cell><m>-\cot x+C</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\sec x\tan x</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\sec x+C</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\csc x\cot x</m></cell>
        <cell><m>-\csc x+C</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\tan x</m></cell>
        <cell><m>-\ln|\cos x|+C</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\cot x</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\ln|\sin x|+C</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\sec x</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\ln|\sec x+\tan x|+C</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>\csc x</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\ln|\csc x-\cot x|+C</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <p>For constants <m>k\neq0</m>,</p>

  <p><me>\int \sin(kx)\,dx=-\frac1k\cos(kx)+C,</me></p>

  <p>and</p>

  <p><me>\int \cos(kx)\,dx=\frac1k\sin(kx)+C.</me></p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="appsubsec-inverse-trig-integral-forms">
    <title>Inverse trigonometric forms</title>

  <p>For <m>a&gt;0</m>, the following patterns are especially common.</p>

  <table xml:id="tab-sec-f-2-integral-formulas-3">
    <title>Inverse trigonometric forms</title><tabular halign="left" width="100%">
      <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
        <cell><alert>integrand</alert></cell>
        <cell><alert>antiderivative</alert></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\displaystyle \frac1{a^2+x^2}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle \frac1a\arctan\left(\frac{x}{a}\right)+C</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\displaystyle \frac1{\sqrt{a^2-x^2}}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle \arcsin\left(\frac{x}{a}\right)+C</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\displaystyle \frac1{x^2-a^2}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle
\frac1{2a}\ln\left|\frac{x-a}{x+a}\right|+C</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\displaystyle \frac1{a^2-x^2}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle
\frac1{2a}\ln\left|\frac{a+x}{a-x}\right|+C</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\displaystyle \frac1{\sqrt{x^2+a^2}}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle
\ln\left|x+\sqrt{x^2+a^2}\right|+C</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle \frac1{\sqrt{x^2-a^2}}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\displaystyle
\ln\left|x+\sqrt{x^2-a^2}\right|+C</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <p>These formulas often appear after completing the square.</p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="appsubsec-trig-substitution-patterns">
    <title>Trigonometric substitution patterns</title>

  <p>For <m>a&gt;0</m>, the following substitutions match the Pythagorean identities.</p>

  <table xml:id="tab-sec-f-2-integral-formulas-4">
    <title>Trigonometric substitution patterns</title><tabular halign="left" width="100%">
      <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
        <cell><alert>expression</alert></cell>
        <cell><alert>substitution</alert></cell>
        <cell><alert>identity used</alert></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\sqrt{a^2-x^2}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>x=a\sin\theta</m></cell>
        <cell><m>1-\sin^2\theta=\cos^2\theta</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><m>\sqrt{a^2+x^2}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>x=a\tan\theta</m></cell>
        <cell><m>1+\tan^2\theta=\sec^2\theta</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>\sqrt{x^2-a^2}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>x=a\sec\theta</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\sec^2\theta-1=\tan^2\theta</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <p>After using a trigonometric substitution, convert the answer back to <m>x</m> unless the
problem asks for a trigonometric parameter.</p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="appsubsec-partial-fraction-patterns">
    <title>Partial fraction patterns</title>

  <p>Partial fractions are used for rational functions.  Factor the denominator first.</p>

  <table xml:id="tab-sec-f-2-integral-formulas-5">
    <title>Partial fraction patterns</title><tabular halign="left" width="100%">
      <col width="50.0%"/><col width="50.0%"/><row header="yes" bottom="medium">
        <cell><p><alert>denominator factor</alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p><alert>partial fraction form</alert></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Distinct linear factor <m>x-a</m></p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>\displaystyle \frac{A}{x-a}</m></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Repeated linear factor <m>(x-a)^m</m></p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>\displaystyle
\frac{A_1}{x-a}
+
\frac{A_2}{(x-a)^2}
+\cdots+
\frac{A_m}{(x-a)^m}</m></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell><p>Irreducible quadratic factor <m>ax^2+bx+c</m></p></cell>
        <cell><p><m>\displaystyle
\frac{Ax+B}{ax^2+bx+c}</m></p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p>Repeated irreducible quadratic factor <m>(ax^2+bx+c)^m</m></p></cell>
        <cell><p>Use one numerator <m>A_jx+B_j</m> over each power
<m>(ax^2+bx+c)^j,
\qquad j=1,\ldots,m.</m></p></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <p>If the degree of the numerator is at least the degree of the denominator, divide first.</p>
  <p>For inverse hyperbolic antiderivatives, including the negative square-root branch and reciprocal patterns, see <xref ref="subsec-inverse-hyperbolic-integrals" text="title"/>. For absolute and conditional improper convergence, see <xref ref="subsec-absolute-conditional-improper-integrals" text="title"/>.</p>
</subsection>

</section>
